 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of sample points, as each point requires a constant-time lookup and interpolation via `grid_sample` [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(H \times W \times D)$ for storing the alpha volume, where $H, W, D$ are the dimensions of the volume grid [S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Highly efficient on GPU due to the use of PyTorch's optimized `grid_sample` kernel [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement, leverages existing deep learning frameworks, efficient for large numbers of samples [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires precomputation of the alpha volume, limited to trilinear interpolation unless modified [S3, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** When ray filtering based on opacity is required and the opacity field is known in advance [S2, unofficial].
-*   **When Not to Use:** When dynamic opacity changes are needed per frame without recomputing the volume, or when higher-order interpolation is required [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity**: $O(N)$ for $N$ query points, dominated by the grid sampling operation which is typically optimized on GPU [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity**: $O(D \times H \times W)$ for storing the alpha volume, where $D, H, W$ are the dimensions of the volume [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Performance**: Efficient for large numbers of samples due to hardware-accelerated grid sampling [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths**: Differentiable, easy to integrate into PyTorch pipelines, supports arbitrary query points [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations**: Requires precomputation of the alpha volume [S1, unofficial]. Accuracy depends on the resolution of the alpha volume and the interpolation method (trilinear) [S3, unofficial].
+*   **When to use**: When ray filtering based on opacity is needed and the alpha volume is already computed [S2, unofficial].
+*   **When not to use**: When memory is constrained and the alpha volume is too large, or when higher-order interpolation is required [S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Alpha Grid Mask is used in neural radiance field (NeRF) and tensor factorization based rendering methods to incorporate opacity masks for better scene representation and ray filtering [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial]. It is commonly found in implementations of TensorRF and related architectures where spatial constraints are applied to the alpha channel [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
+Alpha Grid Mask is a component found in implementations of Tensorial Radiance Fields and related neural rendering frameworks [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. It leverages PyTorch's `grid_sample` function to provide a convenient interface for sampling from 3D grids [S2, unofficial]. The method is used in contexts where opacity information needs to be efficiently queried during volume rendering or novel view synthesis [S1, unofficial][S3, unofficial].
 
